WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:11.859
So, we've talked a little bit about the base entry point, the uWidget, but let's go on to one of the classes that we're probably going to be working with a little bit more, and that's the UserWidget.

2
00:00:11.859 --> 00:00:18.460
So UserWidget is the first blueprint usable class for Unreal widgets.

3
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:27.379
If we're going to try and compare it to the general Unreal framework, I suppose this would be the actor of the UMG world.

4
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:39.679
And whilst it does extend off of UWidget, it's really with UserWidget that we'll start developing our own UI implementations,

5
00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:43.880
and you can even extend off UserWidget for C++ to build your own custom elements.

6
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:51.079
It's not purely a blueprint thing, but within C++, you do have the option to extend directly from UWidget.

7
00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.079
Within Blueprint, you literally cannot make UWidgets.

8
00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.920
You have to start at UWidget and downwards.

9
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.319
So UWidgets can be created at runtime on demand with a templated createWidget call.

10
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:09.319
The syntax is relatively similar to newObject.

11
00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:20.319
So you would pass in, you call createWidget, it's a generic templated type, so you would specify the type that you want to kind of cast this to.

12
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:23.920
Then you've specified the kind of owner for this widget.

13
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.519
So unlike newObject, where you kind of pass in an outer and that outer could be kind of anything,

14
00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:38.319
there's a kind of specific subset of things that are feasible owners for a UMG widget.

15
00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:43.920
So that is another widget, quite obviously, like sometimes you'll be spawning widgets within widgets.

16
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.120
If you think about something like a health bar, you might have segments that represent the individual bits of health.

17
00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:55.609
So it makes quite a lot of sense that health bar, that widgets can create widgets themselves.

18
00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:00.209
A widget tree, which is essentially a data structure keeping track of how widgets are nested within each other.

19
00:02:00.209 --> 00:02:05.010
Think like an inventory window consisting of many inventory slots.

20
00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:09.810
A player controller, which is probably the most common, to be perfectly honest.

21
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:15.909
When you're certainly when you're doing this in a kind of gameplay level, that is the most common thing that you're going to be passing in.

22
00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:21.310
You can spawn them through, you can pass in the game instance as an owner of a widget as well.

23
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.509
To be honest, it's not something I've used myself a huge amount.

24
00:02:24.509 --> 00:02:30.110
So I wouldn't be certain whether or not that means that the widget would persist between scenes.

25
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:36.009
That's something that is worth experimenting with, but it is certainly a valid owner.

26
00:02:36.009 --> 00:02:38.210
And the last option would be the world.

27
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:44.389
So if you try and pass things up, it isn't one of those things, like just a normal actor, you'll probably get told off.

28
00:02:44.389 --> 00:02:50.990
And then the last argument to pass in here is the actual class for the widget you want to be spawning.

29
00:02:50.990 --> 00:03:00.289
So this class should at least be at the same level or a child of the kind of cast to type.

30
00:03:00.289 --> 00:03:05.590
Because otherwise that's going to cause you problems, unsurprisingly.

31
00:03:06.289 --> 00:03:14.490
And unlike UObject, the user widgets keep track of the game world and the local player control.

32
00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.389
I mean, we can kind of pull meaningful information from those things at runtime.

33
00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:25.590
It's very useful that natively any widget will have access to the local player controller that they are displayed on.

34
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:33.090
Obviously, a widget without any access to a player controller doesn't really make sense because widgets only exist to be displayed on a player screen.

35
00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.789
So yeah, that gives us access to kind of once we have access to the player controller,

36
00:03:38.789 --> 00:03:46.090
we can access things they are possessing and then we can easily access things like health components or inventories, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

37
00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:49.770
So you can understand the benefits there.

38
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.669
So user widget has some kind of critical functions that are worth mentioning.

39
00:03:53.669 --> 00:04:00.270
These are the kind of standard things you would first be using when working with these to just get it working out the door.

40
00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.870
So first off, we've got add to viewport.

41
00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:08.969
This kind of does, well, really all of these functions probably do what they say on the tin because they're not terribly named functions.

42
00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:14.469
So this adds the widget to the local player's viewport and it takes in a Z order as a parameter.

43
00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:19.870
This is essentially confusingly because Unreal uses X as depth.

44
00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:27.269
But here, when we're talking about Z, we're talking about the more typical use of Z within a lot of game engines where Z means depth.

45
00:04:27.269 --> 00:04:32.170
So our Z order, as the number goes up, it's going to be rendered on top.

46
00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:40.259
And if these two things are overlapping, the higher number is drawn over the widgets with the lower number.

47
00:04:40.259 --> 00:04:43.060
Add to player screen achieves kind of the same result.

48
00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:49.459
And when we're in a single player game, add to player screen and add to viewport will be almost entirely indistinguishable.

49
00:04:49.459 --> 00:04:53.459
This becomes more relevant when we're in a split screen multiplayer scenario

50
00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:57.660
because each player will get a kind of subdivided part of the screen.

51
00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:07.160
So if we've got like a left right split, player one is probably going to have like the kind of left half of the screen and player two is going to have the right half of the screen.

52
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.160
When you add to player screen and that widget goes into the top left,

53
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:16.660
if you're adding that widget to the second player screen, it's not going to go into the top left of the overall window.

54
00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:22.819
It's going to go into the kind of like top center of the screen because that's the top left of player two's screen.

55
00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:33.920
RemoveFromViewport is a function that you may still occasionally see in tutorials, but it is actually deprecated since Unreal 5.1.

56
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.319
It really does the same thing as RemoveFromParent now.

57
00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:40.720
It does still exist in C++, but all it does is then call RemoveFromParent.

58
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:48.220
So it's just worth highlighting that it will be mentioned by other resources, but it actually doesn't really do anything anymore and it's advised not to use.

59
00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:54.120
And instead we have RemoveFromParent, which is basically just doing the opposite of add to viewport or add to player screen.

60
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.420
This just removes it from whatever it's inside of.

61
00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:03.019
So that could be the viewport itself or it could be from inside of another widget if we're spawning that widget with inside other widgets.
